<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edo people, also called the Bini, are an ethnic group of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. They founded and formed the core population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, whose capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> institution remain central to Edo identity, along with the celebrated Benin bronzes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-people.jpg" alt="Edo people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ebameful · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edo_Trads_(Traditional_Attire).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Edo speak the Edo language, which belongs to the Edoid group, and are the descendants of the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a>. Other Edoid peoples, among them the Esan, the Etsakọ, the Isoko, the Owan and the Urhobo, as well as some other southern groups, migrated from the Benin kingdom. Most of these related groups trace their history back to the old city centre of the Benin people, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>.</p><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> is a Portuguese corruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Ubini</a>, a word that came into use during the reign of the Oba Ewuare, around 1440. Ubini was later corrupted to Bini by the mixed ethnicities living together at the centre, and then to Benin around 1485, when the Portuguese began trading with Ewuare and gave him coral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, which the Edo call ivie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> takes its name from the Edo and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, their central homeland. The state was formerly part of the old Bendel State, also called the Mid-Western Region, and its culture reflects that of the Edo, Urhobo, Esan and related peoples. The Edo are prominent in the Edo South senatorial district, which comprises seven local government areas.</p><p>In traditional belief an invisible world of supernatural beings acts as intercessor for the human world, and offerings are made to them at shrines. Osanobua is the creator and supreme god. His child Olokun rules the waters and gives prosperity and fertility, and another son, Ogun, is the patron god of metalworkers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a>, the god of thunder, and Osun, the god of medicine, are also honoured.</p><p>Coral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> mark Edo dress at weddings and coronations. Women wear a beaded cape or blouse called the ewu-ivie, necklaces called ivie-uru and bracelets called ivie-obo, while men wear a striped cloth over a white shirt and a cap or headdress, which may be made of a mesh of coral beads. Traditional art consists of sculptures, plaques and masks, including the mask of the Queen Mother Idia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>, which are now dispersed through museums around the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edo people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Benin Bronzes are several thousand metal plaques and sculptures that decorated the royal palace in Benin City, made by artists of the <b>Edo people</b> from the fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/work/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Known almost entirely from <b>Edo</b> oral tradition and archaeology.</blockquote><a href="/state/igodomigodo-ogiso-dynasty">Igodomigodo (Ogiso dynasty)</a></li><li><blockquote>Agbor lies in the forest between Benin City and the Niger, among the Ika, an Igboid-speaking people with strong ties to the <b>Edo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-agbor">Kingdom of Agbor</a></li></ul></section>
